Parking Lot :-

Functional:

Vehicles: Bike, Car, Truck
Spot types: Small, Medium, Large
Bike → Small spot, Car → Medium, Truck → Large
Entry gate generates a ticket with timestamp + spot
Exit gate calculates bill (hourly × vehicle type rate)
Multiple floors, multiple spots per floor
Multiple entry + exit gates

Non-Functional (always mention these — shows maturity):

System should be extensible — easy to add new vehicle types
Concurrent entries should be handled (thread safety on spot assignment)


ENTITY              WHY IT EXISTS
─────────────────────────────────────────────
Vehicle             Abstract — base for all vehicles
  └─ Bike           VehicleType = SMALL
  └─ Car            VehicleType = MEDIUM  
  └─ Truck          VehicleType = LARGE

ParkingSpot         A single spot on a floor
  └─ SmallSpot      fits Bike
  └─ MediumSpot     fits Car
  └─ LargeSpot      fits Truck

ParkingFloor        Has list of ParkingSpots

ParkingLot          Has list of ParkingFloors
                    Singleton — only ONE exists

Gate (abstract)     Common gate behavior
  └─ EntryGate      assigns spot, generates ticket
  └─ ExitGate       calculates bill, frees spot

Ticket              Created at entry
                    has: ticketId, vehicle,
                    spot, entryTime

Payment             Created at exit
                    has: ticket, amount, mode

PricingStrategy     Interface — calculates fare
  └─ HourlyPricing
  └─ FlatPricing




Singleton    → ParkingLot (only one exists)
Strategy     → PricingStrategy (Hourly / Flat / VehicleTypeBased),Payment Strategy ->upi,card,
Factory      → SpotFactory (gives right spot for vehicle type)
Observer     → Spot status change → DisplayBoard updates




What you CANNOT do in interface
interface Vehicle {

    String licensePlate; // ❌ ERROR

}

Why?

Because interfaces are not meant to store per-object data.

Why interface avoids state

Because interface is mainly for:

behavior contract

Example:

interface Payable {
    void pay();
}




